<p>Today's brief covers stories published on 28 and 29 October that yesterday's <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-10-29/">morning brief</a> and <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-10-29-2/">update</a> did not include.</p>

<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Applications</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>ServiceNow<em>AI targets up, shares down</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Cloud</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Google Cloud<em>revenue doubled</em></span><span class="hl">AWS<em>beat, cash flow negative</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Silicon</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Samsung HBM<em>record profit</em></span><span>FuriosaAI RNGD<em>inference chips</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Infrastructure security</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">ESX zero-day<em>hypervisor logging</em></span><span>PHP2Shell + Fragnesia<em>chained to root</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Where today's stories land. Money is flowing down the stack to memory and chips, while attackers work at the hypervisor and kernel layer.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. Google Cloud doubles, but Alphabet's cash flow turns negative</h2>
<p>Alphabet reported Google Cloud revenue of about $31 billion for the quarter, roughly double a year earlier, while free cash flow was negative as capital spending on AI infrastructure rose<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. CNBC reported the shares were heading for a record post-earnings loss on 29 October<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> demand for cloud AI is real, and so is the bill for building it. Providers carrying that much capex will push for long commitments and premium AI services. Before signing multi-year deals, know which workloads truly need a specific provider's models or TPUs and which could move. Our note on <a href="/insights/ai-capacity-crunch-cloud-planning/">planning around the AI capacity crunch</a> covers how to keep that leverage.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. AWS beats estimates as Amazon's free cash flow hits minus $42.4 billion</h2>
<p>Amazon's third-quarter results showed AWS revenue ahead of estimates, but trailing free cash flow of negative $42.4 billion; the company held its 2026 capital spending forecast unchanged and shares fell in extended trading<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> two of the three largest clouds are now spending more cash than they make to build AI capacity. That does not threaten service, but it does shape commercial behaviour. Expect committed-spend discounts to be tied to AI products, and make sure your <a href="/insights/focus-finops-ai-cost-allocation/">AI cost allocation</a> is good enough to tell which teams are driving the spend before renewal season.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. Samsung posts a record quarter on AI memory</h2>
<p>Samsung Electronics confirmed record third-quarter operating profit of about 107.4 trillion won on 29 October, driven by its chip business and high-bandwidth memory for AI servers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. The Next Web reports the memory unit set records for both sales and profit on server demand and higher prices<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> memory, not only GPUs, is now a cost driver for AI hardware, and server memory prices have risen sharply this year. If you are buying on-premises inference kit, as in <a href="/insights/private-llm-on-prem/">private LLMs on-premises</a>, get quotes early and size models to the memory you can actually buy. Techniques such as <a href="/insights/speculative-decoding-private-llms/">speculative decoding</a> and quantisation get more valuable when memory is the expensive part.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. Korea backs FuriosaAI's inference chips with a $620 million round</h2>
<p>FuriosaAI closed a $620 million Series D to scale its RNGD inference chip, which it says is in mass production, and to fund a third-generation chip; Korea's National Growth Fund is the largest investor<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. Jon Peddie Research notes the company's performance claims come without published test conditions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> alternatives to Nvidia for inference are maturing, and governments are funding them for sovereignty reasons. Keep your serving stack portable, for example behind <a href="/insights/llm-inference-serving-vllm/">vLLM</a> and an <a href="/insights/kubernetes-inference-gateway/">inference gateway</a>, so you can benchmark a new accelerator on your own workloads rather than trusting vendor numbers.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>5. ServiceNow raises its AI target and suffers a record share fall</h2>
<p>ServiceNow reported third-quarter subscription revenue up 21% and raised its 2026 target for AI annual contract value to $1.7 billion<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>. The shares still fell about 19% in trading on 29 October, which Yahoo Finance links to investor fears that AI agents will erode traditional software seats<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9">9</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> the market is betting that agents will change how enterprise software is priced and bought. For buyers, that means more leverage on per-seat renewals and more vendors bundling agents. Ask vendors how pricing works when an agent does the work of a seat, and test their agents against your own <a href="/insights/evaluating-ai-agents-reliability/">reliability evaluations</a> before paying for them.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>6. Attackers chain an ESX zero-day and PHP2Shell to get root</h2>
<p>Help Net Security reports that a China-nexus group used a VMware ESX logging flaw, fixed by Broadcom in July 2026, as a zero-day to hide its activity, and separately that attackers chained the PHP2Shell flaws with a Linux kernel bug called Fragnesia to gain root on servers and firewalls<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-10">10</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> PHP2Shell is no longer just a web-tier problem once it is chained to a kernel flaw. If you have not finished patching after <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-10-29/">Tuesday's warning</a>, do it now, patch kernels too, and check that hypervisor logs are shipped somewhere an attacker on the host cannot edit. Our <a href="/case-studies/legacy-php-estate-security-modernisation/">legacy PHP estate case study</a> shows how to triage an older estate quickly.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Confirm ESX hosts, PHP runtimes and Linux kernels are on patched versions, and ship hypervisor logs off-host.</li>
<li>List cloud commitments due for renewal in the next year and identify which workloads could move.</li>
<li>Get updated quotes for any planned GPU or memory purchases; prices are moving.</li>
<li>Ask SaaS vendors how agent-based pricing will work before your next renewal.</li>
</ul>
<p>Building in Korea, the US or anywhere else? See <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>, the <a href="/fractional-cto-united-states/">US page</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
